I nmcernetaaslinisg ugelosbhalvceotnricgegrenrsedovaerg eanterdgeyalcorifsiast aentdioen ivnirtohne- development of new technologies for sustainable and clean energy. Hydrogen as abundant, secure, and renewable alternative energy carrier can meet the growing global energy requirement, and the wide application of hygrogen only can be realized through its efficient, low-cost, and environmentalfriendly production.1,2 Electrochemical reduction of water to molecular hydrogen is a highly attractive approach to satisfy these requirements. Among the many hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) electrocatalysts, platinum $\left(\mathrm{Pt}\right)$ gives the best performance, but its application is greatly limited by the scarcity and high cost. Searching and developing high-performance, low cost alternatives to the noble metal electrocatalysts serves as one ideal channel to enable the hydrogen economy. Toward this end, great advances for replacement of Pt-based catalysts have been achieved owing to the development of series of electrocatalysts with good catalytic performance and low cost,3−5 such as transition-metal sulfides,6−10 selenide,11,12 carbides,13−18 phosphides,19−23 oxide,24,25 and nitrides.26,27 However, most of the catalysts currently being developed rely on metal−H bond interaction for the HER, which generally suffers from corrosion and passivation under the extreme environment of strong acid and alkali. Metal nanoparticles encapsulated by carbon layers $\left(\mathbf{M}\ @\mathbf{C}\right)$ are promising nanostructures with high chemical durability, as the encapsulating carbon layers can protect the inner nanoparticles from electrolyte, thus avoid the corrosion and oxidization from external environment as well as prevent the agglomeration with neighboring nanoparticles. Few carbon-layers encapsulated metal nanoparticles have been confirmed to be able to catalyze the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and HER.28−30
However, in these structures, the outermost carbon layer is less modified by the inner core and failed to provide enough active sites, resulting in dissatisfying catalytic activity.31−33 On the basis of current situation, it is essiential for us to modify the external carbon cages aiming at providing enough active sites in the HER process. It is well documented that electronic structure of graphene can be effiectivly modified by chemical replacement of some carbon atoms with heteroatoms, such as nitrogen (N), boron (B), sulfur (S), and phosphorus (P). In particular, the codoping of two elements in reverse electronegativity to that of carbon, e.g., B and N, could activate adjacent carbon atoms by the so-called synergistic coupling effect between two heteroatoms and, consequently, greatly enlarge catalytic active surface area.34−36 And thus, it is anticipated that the carbon cages of $\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}$ can be tailored by the codoping of B and N, which will produce additional active sites to enhance the HER performance. However, to our knowledge, modification of ultrathin carbon shell (carbon layers $^{<3}$ ) in $\mathbf{M}@\mathbf{C}$ structure is a challenging task and has rarely been experimentally realized.
Herein, we develop a bottom-up strategy for the construction of N and B codoped graphitic carbon cages that encapsulate Co nanoparticle $(\mathrm{Co}@\bar{\mathrm{BCN}})$ as HER electrocatalyst by using MOF and $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathrm{{BO}}_{3}$ as the precursors. ZIF-67 ( $\mathrm{\cdot\mathrm{ZIF}=}$ zeolite imidazolate frameworks),37 as a well-known subfamily of MOFs formed through the coordination interaction between the cobalt ions and the imidazole derivatives, was selected as one of the most ideal candidate owing to its merits including abundant nitrogen component and orderly arrangement of cobalt in the framework, which is an appropriate precursor to yield N-doped carbon shells and sheets in the hybrid of ${\mathrm{Co}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ . At the same time, B was introduced by the pyrolysis of $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathrm{{BO}}_{3}$ at the high temperature $(750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Briefly, ZIF-67 was mixed with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ and heated at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar gas, accompanied by the collapse of the ZIF-67 framework as well as the formation of new components (Scheme 1). Then, the temperature was increased to $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the final product of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ hybrid. The formation of graphitic carbon around the surface of cobalt nanoparticles occurs through the dissolution of amorphous carbon into cobalt nanoparticles, accompanied by surface diffusion of N and B atoms, and final assembly into N, B-doped graphitic carbon shell. Significantly, this is the first report on the codoping of N and B in the carbon cages that encapsulating nanoparticles. The coupling between chemical doped carbon cage and the inner cobalt nanoparticle offers excellent catalytic activities toward HER.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of Morphology and Structure. The synthesis process for the Co@BCN hybrid is illustrated in Scheme 1. Uniform polyhedrons of ZIF-67 were constructed by a simple solution-phase method (Figure S2, S3).38,39 Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observation reveals that $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ shrank obviously from the original morphology due to the decomposition of the framework during the calcination aThe framework of ZIF-67 was collapsed after calcination at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar atmosphere accompanied by the formation of new components, and the final $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ hybrid was produced under heating at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the second step.
process. This also suggests that the ZIF-67 served as both a precursor and a self-sacrificing template for the formation of the new hybrid, which was constructed by nanographitic carbon sheet and numerous ultrafine nanospheres with an average diameter of around $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure $^{\mathrm{1b,c}}$ and Figure S4). Further high resolution (HR) TEM analysis confirms that the nanospheres are consisted of metal nanoparticles (NPs) that are completely encapsulated by rather thin carbon cages in $_{1-3}$ layers (Figure 1d). The lattice fringes can be clearly seen in the HRTEM images, in which the 2.09 Å can be assigned to the (111) inter plane space of Co (Figure 1e), and the 3.45 Å belongs to (002) plane of graphene (Figure 1f). The essence of the doping in the hybrid can be disclosed by the elemental mapping images of C, N, B and Co (Figure $2\mathrm{a-f})$ . As expected, B and N atoms are distributed homogeneously over the carbon sheets and carbon shells. Similar structures have also been obtained with the same annealing strategy for reference compounds $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{CN}$ and Co@C. Co@CN was derived from ZIF-67 with the doping of N, and ${\mathrm{Co}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ was synthesized from MOF Co-MOF-74 which contains neither N nor B (Figure S5−S6). Catalyst $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ which was constructed by naked cobalt particles dispersed in carbon sheet has also been developed by direct calcination of Co-MOF-74 at higher temperature (Figure S7).
The nature of doping in hybrid $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ was identified by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) with peak deconvolution (Figure S8). XPS investigation of the Co 2p spectrum reveals the presence of two chemically distinct species: metallic Co $(778.1\ \mathrm{\bar{e}V})$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{III/II}}}$ species peaks at 781.6, 787.3, 796.4, and $804.6\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively, indicating the existence of trace of ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{III}})$ and ${\bf C o}(\mathrm{II})$ originated from surface oxidation of metallic Co in air (Figure 2j).40 The nitrogen content $(\approx3.0\%$ atomic percent) and boron content ( $\approx0.7\%$ atomic percent) in $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ are confirmed by further analysis of the XPS (Figure $2{\mathrm{g-i,}}$ Table S1). The asymmetric N 1s XPS peak of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ (Figure 2f) was deconvoluted into four peaks of pyridinic N $\left(398.7\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ , pyrrolic N $(400.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ graphitic N $(401.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ as well as C−N−B (398.1 eV).10,39,41 Three peaks can be observed by fitting the B 1s XPS spectrum, as shown in Figure ${2}\mathrm{g},$ peaks at ${\sim}189.5$ eV $\left(\mathrm{BC}_{3}\right)$ , ${\sim}190.1$ eV (B−N) and ${\sim}191.2$ $\mathbf{eV}$ ( $\mathrm{BC}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , $B C O_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ) were identified.42 In addition, four distinguishable peaks (284.6, 286.1, 288.6, and $289.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ in the C 1s spectrum (Figure $2\mathrm{~h~}$ ) correspond to the chemical bonding between C atoms and C, N, O, B atoms, respectively.39,41 The XRD spectrum shows that except for the carbon and the Co nanoparticles, no other impurity was present in the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ . Similar XRD patterns were also observed for reference compounds $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{CN}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ (Figure S10). The graphitization degree of carbon phase in different samples are characterized by Raman spectroscopy (Figure S11). The characteristic D band and G band are observed at about 1355 and $1585~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The D band is defect induced Raman features, while the G band is highly dependent on the first-order scattering of the $\mathbf{E}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ mode of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{C}$ domains. The intensity ratio of D band to G band $\left(I_{\mathrm{D}}/\right)$ $I_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{~~}},$ ) increases from 0.96 for ${\mathrm{Co}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ to 1.03 for $\mathrm{Co}\ @\mathrm{CN},$ and 1.09 for $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}_{\cdot}$ , which indicates that relatively high graphitization degree in the carbon phase due to the existence of cobalt nanoparticles during carbonization, also suggesting that the structural disorder of graphene increases upon the Nand B-doping.42,43 The porosity of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}.$ $\operatorname{Co}\varpi\mathrm{CN}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ were measured by $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption−desorption isotherms, all displaying type IV (based on IUPAC classification) isotherms with a hysteresis loop, indicative of the existence of mesopores.44 The specific surface areas of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ , $\operatorname{Co}\varpi\mathrm{CN}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ were 191.25, 210.36, and $142.24~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1},$ respectively, which offered sufficient active sites to facilitate the HER process (Figure S12). As the hybrids were derived from MOFs, and the pore characteristics of MOFs play an important role in determining the pore texture of the resultant porous hybrids. For $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ and $\mathrm{Co}\ @\mathrm{CN},$ , they derived from the identical precursor, that is ZIF-67. The decreased porosity in $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ should be attributed to the partially destroyed ZIF-67 framework during B doping (Figure S13).
Electrocatalytic Performance for HER. Typical threeelectrode setup was applied to evaluate the HER performances of the catalysts, catalyst powders load on glassy carbon was used as working electrode, platinum wire as the counter electrode, and $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ (saturated KCl-filled) as the reference electrode. First, we evaluate the electrocatalytic activity in an Ar-saturated 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ media. For reference, HER performance for commercial Pt catalyst (20 $\mathrm{wt}\ \%\ \mathrm{Pt/C},$ ) has also been investigated. As shown in Figure 3a, blank glassy carbon (GC) electrode, and pristine graphene exhibited negligible or poor HER activities. In contrast, the hybrid catalysts ${\mathrm{Co}}(\varpi C,$ $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{CN}_{\cdot}$ and $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ all exhibited apparent electrocatalytic activities for $\mathrm{HER},$ higher than the naked cobalt particles ${\mathrm{Co/C}},$ which clearly indicated that carbon coating in few layers can effectively modify the work function of cobalt particles and eventually influence their catalytic performances. More importantly, it can be observed that chemical doping greatly enhanced the HER activity of catalyst as indicated by the more and more positive onset potentials from Co@C, Co@CN to $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN},$ respectively.
The $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ showed an onset potential of about $0\mathrm{\:V}$ versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) which is approach to the thermodynamic potential of HER (i.e., 0 V). This onset potential is close to that of $\mathrm{Pt},$ and notably more positive than that of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{CN}$ $\mathrm{(\sim-37~mV)}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathrm{C}}$ $\mathrm{(\sim-82\mV)}$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ $(\sim-186~\mathrm{mV})$ . To achieve a $10\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ HER current density, the dual-doped $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ required a small overpotential of 96 $\mathrm{mV}.$ , much lower than that of single doped $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{CN}$ $\mathrm{{(91~mV)}}$ , nondoped Co@C $(284~\mathrm{mV})$ ) and naked Co particles $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ $(429~\mathrm{\bar{mV}})$ ). These results suggest that $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ is among the most active nonprecious HER electrocatalysts in acidic solution, comparable with cobalt-implanted carbon nanotubes,45,46 WS2 Nanolayers,1 0 Fe/N-rich tungsten carbonitride,47 $\mathrm{Co}_{0.6}\mathrm{Mo}_{1.4}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{,}^{27}$ FeCo-alloy embedded carbon nanotubes,48 FeP nanowire Array,19 cobalt sulfide nanosheet,20 porous molybdenum carbide,13 N,P-codoped graphene,41 as well as Bdoped graphene49 (Table S3).
To investigate the kinetics of HER processes promoted by as-prepared catalysts, we carried out further analysis of the HER performance with the Tafel plots. Mechanistically, three major reactions are typically involved for HER in acid solutions, including a primary discharge step (Volmer reaction, with a Tafel slope of $120\mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), electrochemical desorption step (Heyrovsky reaction, with a Tafel slope of $40\mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}},$ ) or a recombination step of adsorbed hydrogen on catalysts (Tafel reaction, with a Tafel slope of $30\mathrm{^mV\dec^{-1}},$ ). In the present study, $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ gives a Tafel slope of $63.7~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , much lower than that of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{CN}$ $\left(116.3~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\right),$ ), ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ (129.1 $\mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}},$ ), $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{'167.4~mV~dec^{-1}}\$ (Figure 3b). As the ratedetermining step (RDS) is reflected by the Tafel slope of a catalyst, the variation of Tafel slops can be explained by the change of RDS in HER processes. The RDS for $\mathrm{Co}(\varpi\mathrm{CN},$ , $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ C and $\mathrm{Co/C}$ follow the Volmer step, but it is the Heyrovsky step for $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}_{\cdot}$ , that the Volmer−Heyrovsky mechanism can be determined. The exchange current density $\left(j_{0}\right)$ is also one of the most important parameters to describe the catalytic efficiency. By extrapolation of the Tafel plots to J axis, the obtained $j_{0}$ of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ is $0.103\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-\hat{2}}}$ , higher than those of $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{CN}$ $\mathrm{^{prime}0.084\ m A\ c m^{-2}}$ ), and $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}~(0.0\mathrm{.067~mA~cm^{-2}})$ . The highest $j_{0}$ of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ suggests the best catalytic efficiency, further confirming the most favorable HER kinetics at the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ electrolyte interface among all the samples, which is also one of the largest values among reported results.9,10,35,49,50
The HER performance of catalysts in an Ar-saturated basic media was also investigated $(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH})$ ). As shown in Figure 4a, $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{BCN}$ also exhibited good electrocatalytic activity for HER in basic media, polarization curve of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ showed a small overpotential of $183~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs RHE) achieving a significant hydrogen evolution with a current density $(J)$ of $\mathrm{\bar{10}}\mathrm{\m}\mathrm{\Lambda}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ this overpotential comparable with the most active non-noble metal HER electrocatalysts in basic solution, such as transition metal nanoparticle-based catalysts,29,51 N-doped tungsten carbonitride47 as well as copper molybdenum sulfide.52 Similar ranking for the catalytic activity was also observed in alkaline environment, in which $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ outperforms those of many other samples such as $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing\mathbf{C}\mathrm{{N}}$ $(257~\mathrm{mV})$ , Co@C $\mathrm{324~mV)}$ , and $\mathrm{Co/C}$ $\ensuremath{(426~\mathrm{mV})}$ . The $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ catalyst shows a Tafel slope of $73.2~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ in basic solution, which is lower than those of $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{CN}$ $\cdot103.2\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}},$ ), ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ ( $116.5~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1},$ )
and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{^{'}153.7~m V~d e c^{-1}},$ ) catalysts as well (Figure 4b). These results further confirmed that the doping of B and/or N in the out shell of ${\mathrm{Co}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ can effectively produce better performance in both the acid and alkaline media.
Durability is an important parameter in the exploration of electrocatalyst. To investigate the durability of the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ in acidic and alkaline media, long-term potential cycling was performed using polarization curves at an accelerated scanning rate. There was only a slight decrease observed in HER activity after 2000 cycles in acidic or alkaline environment, displaying an excellent durability (Figure 3c and $\mathsf{4c}$ ). Moreover, the practical operations of the catalyst were also examined by electrolysis at fixed potentials over extended periods. As shown in Figure 3d and 4d, the stable current density over $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ of continuous operation at a static overpotential of $0.2\mathrm{V}$ in acidic media and $0.25\mathrm{~V~}$ in alkaline media were presented, which further suggests that the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ hybrid possesses good stability for HER. These performances are much superior to the $\mathrm{Co/C}$ catalyst, which gives only $62\%$ (acidic environment) and $81\%$ (alkaline environment) current density compared with the initial values over $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ continuous operations (Figure S14). The surface compositions of catalyst $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ recycled after electrical catalytic reaction were further characterized by XPS. There was no obvious changes before and after reaction, which clearly confirm the maintenance of the original framework of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ . Besides, TEM observation after reaction has also been investigated, and the representative HRTEM images of the surface regions of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ before and after electrical catalytic reaction are shown in Figure S15. Most of the cobalt nanoparticles are still well coated by carbon layers after reaction, which further confirm the high stability of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ . All these results clearly reveals that the overcoating of carbon layers can effectively avoiding the corrosion and oxidation of the catalyst under extreme acidic and alkaline environment.
Interpretation of the Active Sites. Understanding the interaction between a catalyst and reactant can help us to conclude the reaction mechanism, and also give us paradigm in designing better electrocatalyst for the hydrogen evolution reaction. The enhanced performance of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ is certainly associated with its activated surfance structure. It is our current task to find out the real active sites in the hybrid. To interpret the nature of the active sites of the electrocatalysts, the influence of thiocyanate ions $(\mathrm{SCN^{-}})$ on the HER activity of the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ catalysts was investigated. The metalcentered catalytic sites is widely known to be deactivated by the SCN in acidic conditions. With the introduction of $S C\mathrm{{N}^{-}}$ (5 mM) into the acidic electrolyte, the overpotential over the $\mathrm{Co}/\$ C catalyst at the current intensity of $2\mathsf{0\ m A\ c m}^{-2}$ increased from 541 to ${596}~\mathrm{mV},$ which suggests that a certain amount of cobalt sites were deactivated by the $S C\mathrm{{N}^{-}}$ ions (Figure 5a). More interestingly, there is no significant decrease of HER activity for the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ catalyst in the presence of $S C\mathbf{N}^{-}$ ions (Figure 5b). These results combined with TEM observation, STEM mapping, clearly reveal that B, N codoped carbon shell contributes more significantly to HER as a major active site than trace of uncoated Co species. Electrochemical doublelayers capacitance $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)_{\mathrm{-}}$ , which is believed to be positively proportional to the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), has also been determined by measuring the CV curves in the potential range from 0.04 to $0.14\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs RHE) without redox processes. As can be seen, the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ $\left(0.083\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-2}\right.$ ) is much larger than that of $\mathrm{Co}\ @\mathrm{CN}$ $\left(0.067\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-2}\right)$ and $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ $\mathrm{~C~}(0.051\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-2},$ ) (Figure 5c, d and Figure S16). This should provide direct evidence for the enlarged ECSA over the $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ BCN catalyst.53,54 These results indicate that the number of electrochemically active sites for HER was boosted significantly due to the codoping of heteroatoms in the hybrid. The enlarged
ECSA can not only provide additional active sites but also facilitate charge transfer in the hybrid. Indeed the electrochemical impedance measurements indicate that $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ has much lower impedance and thus markedly accelerate HER kinetics (Figure S17).
## CONCLUSIONS
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis of ZIF-67. ZIF-67 crystals were synthesized according to a previous reference.55 In the synthesis of ZIF-67, a methanolic solutions $(80~\mathrm{mL})$ of cobalt chloride $(519~\mathrm{mg})$ ), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) $\left(600~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and 2-Methylimidazole (MeIm) $\left(2630~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were mixed under stirring. Then the mixture was kept reaction at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The bright purple powder of ZIF-67 was collected by centrifugation, washed several times with methanol, and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Synthesis of Co-MOF-74. Co-MOF-74 crystals were synthesized according to a previous reference.56 Typically, $\mathrm{CoCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(762~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and 2,5-Dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{{H_{4}D O T})}$ $\left(344\ \mathrm{\mg}\right)$ were dissolved in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF/EtOH/water (in a 15:1:1 ratio respectively). After thorough mixing, the solution was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and compounds were heated to $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20{-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with DMF and ethanol for three times, respectively. At last, the dark purple powder of Co-MOF-74 was obtained after drying in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12{\mathrm{~h}}.$ .
Preparation of the $\mathsf{C o@B C N}_{\mathsf{\Gamma}}$ , $\mathsf{C o@C N},$ , $\mathsf{C o@C}$ and $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C}.$ For the preparation of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN},$ , the powder of ZIF-67 $(500~\mathrm{mg})$ was blended with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ ( $\mathrm{200~mg)}$ in aqueous to guarantee $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBO}_{3}$ was well dispersed on the crystal of ZIF-67, and then the mixture was placed in a tube furnace under Ar gas flow, heated to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\cdot^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ min), and maintained at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This process undergoes the removal of solvent molecule, the collapse of the framework, the formation of new component in nanoscale, and the diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ in gaseous phase. Then the temperature was elevated to $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $^\mathrm{~1~h,~}$ and maintained at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h,}}$ to realize the catalytic graphitization by the active Co catalyst. This two-step calcination resulted in the final $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN}$ core−shell catalyst.
Similar method as described above was used for the preparation of $\mathrm{Co}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{CN},$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\varpi{\mathbb{C}}$ with the precursor of ZIF-67 and Co-MOF-74, respectively, except that $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ was not added. In order to remove the insecure and exposed metal particles, all the synthesized samples were treated in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ followed by a repeated filter and washing process in deionized water. The $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}$ was prepared in a modified method, the powder of $C_{0}$ -MOF-74 $\langle500\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ was placed in a tube furnace under Ar gas flow $\left(2~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}\right)$ , heated directly to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h,}$ and maintained at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Reaction Condition and Activity Evaluation. Three-electrode cell on a CH Instruments Model 650A electrochemical workstation was used to evaluate the electrochemical activities of various catalysts. Linear sweep voltammetry with a $\zeta\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ scan rate was carried out from 0 to $-0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl)}$ in electrolyte solution under continuous purging with Ar. The catalyst was cycled numerous times by CV until a stable CV curve was obtained before testing. For the preparation of the working electrode, $1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ as-synthesized catalyst was mixed with $20~\mu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution ( $5.0\%$ Nafion in ethanol) and $1000~\mu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol. The mixture was sonicated and the suspension was piped out and dropped onto a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) with a diameter of $3\mathrm{mm}$ and then fully dried. All electrodes were prepared by depositing the same mass loading of catalysts on GCE using identical method. Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were evaluated at $-0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE with frequencies from $0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ to $1\times10^{5}~\mathrm{Hz}$ .
Electrochemically Active Surface Area (EASA) Evaluation. The electrochemically active surface area was estimated from the electrochemical double-layer capacitance of the hybrids. The electrochemical double-layer capacitance was determined from the CV curves measured in a potential range without redox processes according to the following equation:
$$
C_{\mathrm{dI}}=I_{\mathrm{c}}/\nu
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{dl}},I_{\mathrm{c}},$ and $\nu$ are the double-layer capacitance $\left(\mathrm{F\cm}^{-2}\right),$ ) of the electroactive materials, charging current $\mathrm{\bar{(mA~cm^{-2})}}$ ), and scan rate $\mathrm{(mV~\thinspaces^{-1})}$ . In the present work, the capacitive currents (taken at the potential of $0.09{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE) as a function of scan rate for $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{B}\mathrm{CN},$ $\mathrm{Co}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{CN},$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ have been investigated.
Characterization. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on an X-Pert diffractometer equipped with graphite monochromatized Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation. The specific surface areas were determined with a surface area analyzer (BEL Sorp-II mini, BEL Japan Co., Japan) by the Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) method. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were taken by a field emission transmission electron microscope (2100F, JEOL Co., Japan) operated at $200~\mathrm{kV}$ . The Co contents of hybrids were analyzed by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES, SPS3520UV-DD, SII nano technology Inc., Japan). Surface chemical analysis was performed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI Quantera SXM, ULVAC-PHI Inc., Japan).
